WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:06.109
An addictive new season of You has arrived
on Netflix as serial stalker Joe heads to

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:10.169
LA in search of a fresh start and perhaps
a true Love story.

00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:11.480
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:16.460
I'm Jan and in this video I'm discussing the
ending of Season 2 of You, revealing all the

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.850
clues that predicted the big twist in the
finale, and explaining what that final shot

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.820
really means for Season 3.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.130
We will be talking spoilers, of course, so
take care if you're not all caught up.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:32.430
The final episode of Season 2, appropriately
titled "Love, Actually", explains the twist

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:37.449
that Love is as dark and warped as Joe and
also a serial killer like him.

00:00:37.449 --> 00:00:39.010
"We're soulmates Joe."

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:43.140
At this point, Love has just murdered Candace
in cold-blood, something foreshadowed right

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.339
from the very first episode when Joe arrives
in LA and sees the body of a red-head girl

00:00:47.339 --> 00:00:49.089
lying in a pool of blood.

00:00:49.089 --> 00:00:53.440
That was just an actress filming a scene,
but the placement of the heart-shaped sunglasses

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.140
and the black leather jacket were the first
sign that Candace wouldn't make it out of

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.239
LA alive.

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:03.739
And as Love reveals how and why she did what
she did, the parallels between her and Joe

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:05.909
become even more striking.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:10.509
Love killed Delilah and Candace, because they
were both obstacles in the way of her forming

00:01:10.509 --> 00:01:15.610
a family with Joe, similar to how Joe killed
Benji and Peach in Season 1 when he saw them

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.799
as being in the way of his relationship with
Beck.

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:23.020
Right from the moment they met, Love latched
on to Joe as a means to fulfil her fantasy

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.590
of having a happy family:
"I knew right away, in my gut, I recognized

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:28.590
something in you.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.090
A little darkness that made you less storybook,
more real.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.110
I started to fantasize again."

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:39.490
In in the first episode, it's Love who makes
a move on Joe, scuppering his plan to accidentally

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.000
bump into her.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.810
And like Joe, Love is able to charm those
around her, something he immediately picks

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:46.810
up on.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.750
"You have a way with people, they just like
you."

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:53.240
Again the same as Joe, Love is perceptive
and she immediately recognises the darkness

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.049
in him and compares it to herself.

00:01:55.049 --> 00:01:56.890
"Yeah, I thought about getting you something
happy.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:02.240
But let's face it, what you're more interested
in is something smart and complex.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.950
A little dark.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.609
That's what makes you feel at home.

00:02:06.609 --> 00:02:08.680
How do you know that?

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:10.660
Well cause me too."

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.909
After Candace reveals the truth about Joe's
real identity to the Quinns, Love begins to

00:02:14.909 --> 00:02:20.060
investigate him more thoroughly, but his dark
past doesn't deter her one bit.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.290
She steals the keys to his creepy lock-up
where she finds his cage and figures out the

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.650
truth about Beck and Joe's relationship.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:33.400
"I devoured your dead ex's book All the articles,
all the think pieces, reading between the

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.400
lines.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.409
And I realized, Guinevere Beck was unspecial
and mediocre.

00:02:37.409 --> 00:02:38.409
She didn't deserve you."

00:02:38.409 --> 00:02:42.230
And it's very similar to how Joe felt about
Beck's original boyfriend Benji.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.850
"I do understand something about you now,
Beck.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.989
You somehow think this guy is all you deserve."

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:53.540
Love even seems to admire Joe's brutal actions
seeing them as a mirror of her own violent

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.540
past.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.750
"Yeah, you did some terrible things but that's
what sensitive people do when they're trapped

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.140
in a bad relationship."

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.780
By the way, Love's affinity for knives and
murder was signalled early on in the season

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.950
when we see her butchering a rack of ribs
in a scene that's intercut with shots of Joe

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.690
chopping up Jasper's dead body.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.879
And Love's preference for slashing her victims
in the throat was also hinted at when she

00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:19.590
fake attacks Joe in the neck with a toy wolf
during her family's wellness weekend.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:25.330
So, Love may be what Joe originally considered
his soulmate, but he actually seems disappointed

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.260
in the love he found in her by the end of
the season, and he ponders this at Lucy and

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.370
Sunrise's wedding.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:35.170
"All I've ever wanted is to love and be loved
by someone real and true.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.370
[Joe] Me too.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:38.129
Is that what we are?
Soulmates?

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:40.950
Is this what real love is?

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.360
Knowing and accepting anything."

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.220
I think his state of unease stems from his
twisted beliefs about romantic love, mixed

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.590
with his confused feelings over whether he's
really a good or bad person.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:56.329
On the one hand, Joe still harbours delusions
of himself as a nice guy and saviour.

00:03:56.329 --> 00:04:01.530
"The hero races to the cage,
he raises the door, and steps inside,

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:06.909
eager to set Delilah free,
and begin the rest of his life."

00:04:06.909 --> 00:04:10.459
And in the first episode, he even claims he's
a changed man.

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:15.640
"I don't fantasize to some impossible version
of a woman I barely know."

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:20.320
However, we quickly see he's repeating similar
patterns to what he did with Beck, and by

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.340
the time of the finale, Love herself accuses
Joe of glossing over the truth with his own

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:26.720
warped version of reality.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.699
"Because while I was seeing you, really seeing
you, you were busy gazing at a goddamn fantasy.

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:36.620
A perfectly imperfect girl.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.780
You saw what you wanted to see."

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.240
In Season 2, Joe keeps making the point that
he's trying to change, especially after a

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:47.830
vision of Beck confronts him over her murder
in the second episode.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:53.780
Unlike Benji who he caged then killed in season
1, he lets his first captive Will go free,

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:57.460
and the two murders he does commit don't seem
to be premeditated.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:02.050
The first is in self-defence when Jasper attacks
him, and Henderson was pushed down the stairs

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:03.419
as he tried to escape.

00:05:03.419 --> 00:05:07.530
As Joe's original plan was to have Henderson
confess his crimes on camera.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:12.840
But we have to remember that much of what
we see in You is presented through Joe's point-of-view

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.590
and that he is an unreliable narrator who
spends a good deal of time rationalising his

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.449
own often creepy and violent behaviour.

00:05:20.449 --> 00:05:25.030
Joe may have saved Ellie from Henderson, but
after he traps the sleazy celebrity, he's

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:29.740
forced to consider how much difference there
really is between Henderson and himself.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:34.389
Joe believes his actions are noble, but Henderson
quickly dismantles that illusion.

00:05:34.389 --> 00:05:36.940
"You feel like you're a piece of shit so you're
trying to be a hero.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:39.500
Ruining my life is not gonna save you from
your demons, all right?"

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:40.610
We're the same.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:43.260
[Joe] No, no...

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.240
I'm nothing... like you."

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:50.509
We know of course that Joe has manipulated,
drugged, knocked out, and trapped people to

00:05:50.509 --> 00:05:53.860
fulfil his own desires and he's even killed
them.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:59.169
Henderson has done similar, minus the killing,
except he's targeted underage girls.

00:05:59.169 --> 00:06:00.580
So who is worse?

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:05.490
It's a difficult and unsettling question,
that Joe is definitely not willing to address.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:06.830
"We're nothing alike.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.389
He's trying to win with words and ideas that
add up to nothing.

00:06:10.389 --> 00:06:12.110
He is bad.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:13.732
Someone needs to stop him and he knows it."

00:06:13.732 --> 00:06:19.270
In a way, Henderson and Love are two sides
of the same coin for Joe as they both force

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:24.360
him to look at himself and reflect on his
own actions and he feels threatened and absolutely

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:25.760
terrified by that.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.059
"No, this isn't me.

00:06:27.059 --> 00:06:28.800
This isn't me anymore.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.560
I didn't want to do that.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.230
I didn't do that."

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:37.211
As co-creator Sera Gamble has said, part of
what they're exploring in the show is "the

00:06:37.211 --> 00:06:42.340
ultimate question of how self-aware Joe can
get" and even the showrunners say they "don't

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:45.210
really know the ultimate answer to that yet."

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:50.110
Joe's inability to properly confront and deal
with his past crimes continues through the

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:51.110
season.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:56.340
In the sixth episode, he plans to go to Candace's
Airbnb to abduct her and presumably kill her

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:57.509
once and for all.

00:06:57.509 --> 00:07:02.220
And in the eighth episode, when Forty pretends
to be Beck to re-enact her murder, Joe begins

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.840
to hear Beck's voice accusing him and he can't
handle it.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:07.289
"[Beck] You're not special.

00:07:07.289 --> 00:07:08.289
You're broken.

00:07:08.289 --> 00:07:09.830
I could never love you.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.530
You are the bad thing.

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.810
You are the thing that you should have killed."

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:18.789
So he begins to strangle Forty for real just
as he did with Beck.

00:07:18.789 --> 00:07:22.419
Then later, when Forty asks Joe
"How do you think Dr Nicky hid his sociopathy

00:07:22.419 --> 00:07:23.419
for so long?"

00:07:23.419 --> 00:07:27.740
Joe justifies the actions of Beck's killer
- that's himself of course - with yet more

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:28.740
self-delusions.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:30.500
"Why does he have to be a sociopath?

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.569
Maybe he's a man who would do anything for
love."

00:07:33.569 --> 00:07:38.060
Later on, a vision of Joe's mother tells him:
"People like us don't change.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:39.060
I've changed.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:43.050
So why are you already thinking of ways to
get rid of Forty."

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:48.139
Like Lady Macbeth, Joe sees his own culpability
and complicity in the deaths of those around

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:50.479
him, in a vision of his own bloodied hands.

00:07:50.479 --> 00:07:54.919
And then when Joe discovers that Forty has
actually cracked a code hidden in Beck's autobiography

00:07:54.919 --> 00:07:59.360
that it was her ex-boyfriend who killed her
and not Dr Nicky, he grabs a piece of glass

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:01.159
and goes to kill Forty.

00:08:01.159 --> 00:08:05.479
He doesn't follow through, but that's only
because he hears about Forty's childhood trauma

00:08:05.479 --> 00:08:08.069
with the family's murdered au pair.

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:12.580
And after Joe thinks that he's killed Delilah
himself, he begins to believe that he deserves

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.520
to be punished:
"I'm done with excuses.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.640
I'm done blaming everyone else for the things
I've done.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.250
But it's time I take responsibility.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:23.250
Accept the punishment for my crime.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:25.640
That a cage is where I belong."

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:30.509
This idea fits in with the literary reference
to Dostoevsky's novel "Crime and Punishment"

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:34.090
which appeared in both the first and last
episodes of Season 2.

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:39.410
"In Crime and Punishment, the hero willingly
walks into exile.

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:43.050
He is killed, but he's also found love.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:45.330
And if he repents, he can be redeemed.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.780
He and the woman he loves can be saved."

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:53.000
Dr Nicky echoes a similar idea about deserving
punishment when Forty goes to see him in prison

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:56.270
with evidence that could prove the therapist
didn't kill Beck.

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:00.700
Dr Nicky refuses to co-operate even though
he knows that he's innocent of that crime,

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:03.980
because he believes he should be punished
nonetheless for his other sins.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:07.570
"I didn't kill Beck, but I am guilty.

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:09.980
I'm guilty of using her.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:11.780
And she's not the first.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.130
No, I'm a bad person.

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:15.020
I'm exactly where I deserve to be."

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:19.530
However, Joe seems willing to switch how he
feels about whether he deserves to punished.

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:23.820
Although he says he's now trapped in a cage,
forced to live with a woman he no longer loves,

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:28.460
which you could see as a sort of fitting punishment,
in the very final scene we see him quickly

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:29.940
revert to his old tricks.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:35.720
"I had no idea that the cage I was building
all this time was a trap for me.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:38.560
But that's not how destiny works, is it?

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:44.290
This is just the beginning...
because this is where I had to be, exactly...

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:46.980
where I had to be to meet... you."

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:52.360
As we can see, Joe has started on a new delusional
path, twisting reality, and pretending to

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:56.870
himself that falling for Candace, Beck and
Love were just errors in a greater scheme,

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:02.400
blips along the path of destiny to meet a
literate and literal girl next door.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:07.530
Now this scene is clearly teasing a setup
for Season 3, and just before I get to that

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:12.400
and explain why we're very likely to get at
least two more seasons before Joe perhaps

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.750
gets the justice he deserves,
I want to let you know that thanks to this

